summary: "I examine the rapid buildout of data centers and identify two distortions that may lead to inefficient siting decisions. I argue that data centers are often treated as the primary problem, even when their direct net harms are limited or context-dependent. More importantly, their rapid expansion reveals broader structural failures in other industries, where concentrated interests can shape decisions in ways that impose costs on society."
